20 Important Linux Commands with Explanations
1. pwd — Print Working Directory
Command
pwd

Explanation
pwd stands for Print Working Directory.
It shows the complete path of the directory you are currently working in.
Useful when you want to know your current location in the Linux filesystem.
Example Output
/home/edukron

2. ls — List Files and Directories
Command
ls

Explanation
Displays files and directories inside the current directory.
It is one of the most commonly used Linux commands.
Example
ls /home

3. ls -la — Detailed File Listing
Command
ls -la

Explanation
-l means long listing format.
-a means show all files, including hidden files.
Hidden files in Linux normally start with ..
It displays permissions, owner, group, size, modification time, and filename.
Example Output
drwxr-xr-x 2 user user 4096 Aug 12 Documents -rw-r--r-- 1 user user 1200 Aug 12 notes.txt

4. cd — Change Directory
Command
cd /var/log

Explanation
cd stands for Change Directory.
Used to move from one directory to another.
Useful Examples
cd .. cd ~ cd /home cd /var/log

cd .. → Move one directory backward.
cd ~ → Go to the user's home directory.
5. mkdir — Create Directory
Command
mkdir project

Explanation
mkdir stands for Make Directory.
Creates a new directory.
Example
mkdir devops

This creates a directory called devops.

6. touch — Create Empty File
Command
touch test.txt

Explanation
Creates a new empty file.
If the file already exists, it updates the file's timestamp.
Example
touch application.log

7. cat — Display File Content
Command
cat test.txt

Explanation
cat stands for concatenate.
Commonly used to display the complete contents of a text file.
Example
cat /etc/os-release

This can be used to check information about the Linux operating system.

8. cp — Copy Files
Command
cp source.txt destination.txt

Explanation
cp stands for Copy.
Copies a file from one location to another.
Example
cp app.log /home/user/backup/

Copy Directory
cp -r project/ backup/

-r means recursively copy the directory and its contents.

9. mv — Move or Rename Files
Command
mv old.txt new.txt

Explanation
mv stands for Move.
It can move files/directories.
It can also rename files/directories.
Rename Example
mv oldname.txt newname.txt

Move Example
mv application.log /var/log/

10. rm — Remove Files
Command
rm test.txt

Explanation
rm stands for Remove.
Deletes files.
Delete Directory
rm -r project/

Force Delete
rm -rf project/

-r → Recursive deletion.
-f → Force deletion without confirmation.
Warning
Be extremely careful with rm -rf because deleted files may not be easily recoverable.

11. df -h — Check Disk Space
Command
df -h

Explanation
df stands for Disk Free.
Displays filesystem disk usage.
-h means human-readable format.
Example Output
Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% /dev/sda1 50G 20G 30G 40%

This tells us:

Total disk = 50 GB
Used = 20 GB
Available = 30 GB
Usage = 40%
12. du -sh — Check Directory Size
Command
du -sh /var/log

Explanation
du stands for Disk Usage.
Used to check how much disk space a directory is consuming.
-s → Summary.
-h → Human-readable.
Example
du -sh /home/user

Example Output
2.5G /home/user

This means the directory consumes approximately 2.5 GB.

13. free -h — Check Memory Usage
Command
free -h

Explanation
Displays RAM and swap-memory usage.
-h displays values in MB/GB.
Example Output
          total    used    free
Mem: 15Gi 2Gi 10Gi Swap: 2Gi 0Gi 2Gi

Useful for checking whether a Linux server is running low on memory.

14. top — Monitor System Processes
Command
top

Explanation
Displays running processes in real time.
Shows CPU usage.
Shows memory usage.
Shows load average.
Shows process IDs.
Helps identify processes consuming excessive resources.
Important columns include:

PID → Process ID
USER → Process owner
%CPU → CPU utilization
%MEM → Memory utilization
COMMAND → Process/program name

Press q to exit.

15. ps aux — Display Running Processes
Command
ps aux

Explanation
Displays detailed information about currently running processes.
Important Columns
USER → Process owner
PID → Process ID
%CPU → CPU utilization
%MEM → Memory utilization
COMMAND → Command that started the process

Find a Particular Process
ps aux | grep nginx

This searches for processes related to Nginx.

16. kill — Stop a Process
Command
kill 1234

Explanation
Stops a running process using its Process ID (PID).
By default, it sends a termination signal to the process.
Example
kill 4567

Force Kill
kill -9 4567

-9 forcefully terminates the process.

Use kill -9 only when normal termination is not working.

17. whoami — Display Current User
Command
whoami

Explanation
Displays the username of the currently logged-in user.
Example Output
edukron

Useful when working with multiple users or switching between normal and root accounts.

18. ip addr — Check IP Address
Command
ip addr

Explanation
Displays network interfaces and IP addresses configured on the Linux machine.
Commonly used for network troubleshooting.
Short Version
ip a

You may see interfaces such as:

eth0
ens33
lo

lo represents the loopback interface.

19. ping — Test Network Connectivity
Command
ping google.com

Explanation
Tests whether your server can communicate with another server.
Helps troubleshoot network connectivity.
It also displays response time.
Example
ping 8.8.8.8

Press Ctrl + C to stop the ping command.

If IP ping works but domain-name ping does not work, there may be a DNS issue.

20. systemctl — Manage Linux Services
Command
systemctl status ssh

Explanation
systemctl is used to manage services on Linux systems using systemd.
You can start, stop, restart, enable, disable, and check services.
Check Service
systemctl status nginx

Start Service
sudo systemctl start nginx

Stop Service
sudo systemctl stop nginx

Restart Service
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Enable Service During Boot
sudo systemctl enable nginx

