20 More Basic Linux Commands — 21 to 40
21. clear — Clear the Terminal Screen
Command
clear

Explanation
Clears all previous output from the terminal screen.
It does not delete commands or files.
It simply gives you a clean terminal screen.
Shortcut
Ctrl + L

22. date — Display Current Date and Time
Command
date

Explanation
Displays the current system date and time.
Also shows the configured timezone.
Example Output
Wed Aug 12 15:55:10 IST 2026

23. cal — Display Calendar
Command
cal

Explanation
Displays the calendar for the current month.
Example
cal

Display a Specific Year
cal 2026

Display a Specific Month
cal 8 2026

24. hostname — Display System Name
Command
hostname

Explanation
Displays the hostname of the Linux machine.
A hostname is the name assigned to a computer/server.
Example Output
devops-server

More Detailed Information
hostnamectl

25. uname — Display System Information
Command
uname

Explanation
Displays basic information about the operating system.
Detailed Command
uname -a

Example
uname -a

This can display:

Kernel name
Hostname
Kernel version
Architecture
Operating system information
26. history — Display Previous Commands
Command
history

Explanation
Displays commands previously executed in the terminal.
Useful when you want to reuse an old command.
Example Output
1 pwd 2 ls 3 cd /var/log 4 df -h 5 free -h

Run Command Number 4 Again
!4

27. echo — Print Text or Variables
Command
echo "Hello Linux"

Explanation
Displays text on the terminal.
Also commonly used to display environment variables.
Example
echo "Welcome to DevOps"

Display Variable
echo $HOME

28. man — Open Command Manual
Command
man ls

Explanation
man stands for Manual.
Displays detailed documentation about a Linux command.
Example
man cp

It explains:

Command syntax
Available options
Description
Usage
Press q to exit the manual.

29. head — Display Beginning of a File
Command
head file.txt

Explanation
Displays the first 10 lines of a file by default.
Example
head /etc/passwd

Display First 5 Lines
head -n 5 file.txt

Useful when working with large files.

30. tail — Display End of a File
Command
tail file.txt

Explanation
Displays the last 10 lines of a file by default.
Very useful for checking log files.
Example
tail /var/log/syslog

Display Last 20 Lines
tail -n 20 file.txt

Monitor a Log File Continuously
tail -f application.log

tail -f is commonly used by DevOps engineers to monitor application logs in real time.

31. less — Read Large Files
Command
less file.txt

Explanation
Opens a file page by page.
Useful when the file is too large for cat.
Example
less /var/log/syslog

Navigation
Space → Next page
b → Previous page
q → Quit
/word → Search for a word

32. grep — Search Text
Command
grep "error" application.log

Explanation
Searches for specific text inside a file.
Example
grep "failed" server.log

Ignore Uppercase/Lowercase
grep -i "error" application.log

Search Processes
ps aux | grep nginx

grep is one of the most commonly used Linux commands for troubleshooting.

33. find — Search for Files
Command
find /home -name "test.txt"

Explanation
Searches for files and directories.
You can search using filename, type, size, and other conditions.
Example
find / -name "application.log"

Find All .txt Files
find /home -name "*.txt"

34. which — Find Command Location
Command
which python

Explanation
Shows where a command or executable is installed.
Example
which python3

Example Output
/usr/bin/python3

Another Example
which docker

35. wc — Count Lines, Words and Characters
Command
wc file.txt

Explanation
wc stands for Word Count.
Displays line count, word count, and character/byte count.
Count Lines
wc -l file.txt

Count Words
wc -w file.txt

Example
wc -l application.log

Useful for checking how many lines are present in a log or data file.

36. sort — Sort File Content
Command
sort names.txt

Explanation
Sorts lines of text alphabetically.
Example File
Ravi Anil Bharath Kiran

Command
sort names.txt

Output
Anil Bharath Kiran Ravi

Reverse Sorting
sort -r names.txt

37. chmod — Change File Permissions
Command
chmod 755 script.sh

Explanation
chmod stands for Change Mode.
Used to change file and directory permissions.
Linux permissions are:

r → Read
w → Write
x → Execute

Example
chmod +x script.sh

This gives execute permission to the script.

Another Example
chmod 644 file.txt

Common permission values:

755 → Owner can read/write/execute; others can read/execute
644 → Owner can read/write; others can only read

38. chown — Change File Owner
Command
sudo chown user file.txt

Explanation
chown stands for Change Owner.
Changes ownership of a file or directory.
Example
sudo chown edukron application.log

Change Owner and Group
sudo chown edukron:developers application.log

Here:

edukron → User/owner
developers → Group

39. sudo — Run Command with Administrative Privileges
Command
sudo apt update

Explanation
sudo stands for Superuser Do.
Allows an authorized user to execute commands with elevated privileges.
Example
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Another Example
sudo mkdir /opt/project

You normally need sudo when changing system configurations, installing software, or managing services.

40. apt — Manage Software Packages
Command
sudo apt update

Explanation
apt is a package management command commonly used in Ubuntu and Debian.
Used to install, update, upgrade, and remove software.
Update Package Information
sudo apt update

Upgrade Installed Packages
sudo apt upgrade

Install Nginx
sudo apt install nginx

Remove Nginx
sudo apt remove nginx

Search for a Package
apt search nginx

